Context
    DocumentVersion: 0.1.0
    GrammarVersion: 1.0.1
    Language: en

Page
    Type: Page
    Role: Content
    Purpose: Full normative specification of the Semantic Text Format (STF) core — the format-independent layer. This document is written in STF; it is both the specification and a conformant STF document demonstrating the format it defines.
    STFSyntax
        Type: Section
        Role: Content
        Caption: 2. STF — Semantic Text Format (SYNTAX)

        Text
            Content: STF is a text format for semantic representation. It is the concrete syntax of the format — the "how to write and read" layer. STF is intentionally designed to be directly interpretable by contemporary language models without task-specific training, fine-tuning, or system prompts. At the same time, it is a formal grammar that can be parsed deterministically by a machine. These two properties are complementary — the parser consumes syntax; the consumer (human or agent) consumes semantics.

        Text
            Content: STF is defined on two levels. Lexical grammar defines valid characters and tokens — what STF is made of. Syntactic grammar defines structural rules and hierarchy — how tokens form a document. Semantic interpretation is defined in §3.

        Text
            Content: A conformant implementation separates parsing from semantic validation. The Parser consumes STF bytes and produces an AST (Abstract Syntax Tree). It checks lexical and syntactic rules only. The Semantic Validator consumes the AST and checks semantic constraints: required objects, Id uniqueness, knowledge zone boundaries, Origin privilege, duplicate properties, and other rules defined in §3-§4. A document that passes the Parser but fails the Validator is syntactically valid STF but semantically invalid. Implementations MUST distinguish SyntaxError (parser) from SemanticError (validator).

        CanonicalSerialization
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 2.0 Canonical Serialization

            Text
                Content: The canonical STF serialization is: UTF-8 encoding without BOM, LF (U+000A) line endings, 4-space indentation using U+0020 only, no trailing whitespace on any line, property order is semantic-free (any order is valid), final newline required. NFC Unicode normalization is RECOMMENDED. NFD and other normalization forms SHOULD be accepted by parsers. UTF-8 BOM (U+FEFF) at the start of the document MUST be rejected.

            Text
                Content: Before parsing, a conformant parser MUST normalize CRLF (backslash-r backslash-n) sequences to LF (backslash-n). A bare backslash-r not followed by backslash-n is treated as a regular character. STF parser limits: maximum property line length 8192 bytes (8 KiB), maximum nesting depth 16 levels. Implementations MUST reject documents exceeding these limits. STF does NOT support escape sequences within property values — the sequences backslash-n, backslash-t, backslash-backslash, backslash-uXXXX are interpreted as literal characters. For multi-line content, use nested Text or Content objects.

        LexicalGrammar
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 2.1 Lexical Grammar

            CharacterClasses
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 2.1.1 Character Classes

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: UPPER_ALPHA
                    Content: "A" | "B" | ... | "Z"

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: LOWER_ALPHA
                    Content: "a" | "b" | ... | "z"

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: ALPHA
                    Content: UPPER_ALPHA | LOWER_ALPHA

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: DIGIT
                    Content: "0" | "1" | ... | "9"

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: UNDERSCORE
                    Content: "_"

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: DASH
                    Content: "-"

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: DOT
                    Content: "."

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: COLON
                    Content: ":"

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: SPACE
                    Content: " "

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: NEWLINE
                    Content: "backslash-n"

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: TAB
                    Content: "backslash-t" — forbidden in indentation (see §2.1.4)

            ObjectName
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 2.1.2 Object Name

                Text
                    Content: An ObjectName identifies a structural node in the STF document. It appears at the start of a line at a given indentation depth, with no ": " separator following it (see §2.2.3 for disambiguation).

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: object_name
                    Content: UPPER_ALPHA { ALPHA | DIGIT | UNDERSCORE }

                Text
                    Content: Valid examples: Context, Page, Article, NavigationMenu, Message_1, UserProfile, CommentEntry, NextPage, Notice, LoginForm, LoadMoreButton.

                Text
                    Content: Invalid examples: context (does not start with uppercase), 2Factor (does not start with a letter), My-Object (dash not allowed), Two Words (space not allowed).

                Text
                    Content: ObjectName is used as a human-readable structural identifier in the STF document, a navigation anchor within the document tree, and the target in action requests for objects with Id (see §3.4). Maximum length: 256 characters. Implementations MUST reject documents containing ObjectNames exceeding this limit.

            PropertyKey
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 2.1.3 Property Key

                Text
                    Content: A PropertyKey labels a key-value pair within an object. Standard keys are pure PascalCase without underscores. Vendor keys use reverse-domain prefix to prevent collisions.

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: property_key
                    Content: standard_key | vendor_key

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: standard_key
                    Content: UPPER_ALPHA { ALPHA | DIGIT } (* Pure PascalCase, no underscores. Any PascalCase token is a grammatically valid property key. The standard keys defined by this specification are listed in §3.2. Unknown PascalCase keys are accepted by the parser and handled per §5.2 Unknown Extensions. *)

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: vendor_key
                    Content: reverse_domain "." UPPER_ALPHA { ALPHA | DIGIT } (* com.example.CustomField *)

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: reverse_domain
                    Content: LOWER_ALPHA { LOWER_ALPHA | DIGIT } { "." LOWER_ALPHA { LOWER_ALPHA | DIGIT } }

                Text
                    Content: Difference from ObjectName: standard property keys do NOT contain underscores (pure PascalCase — MinLength, not Min_Length). A property key MUST be followed by ": " (colon-space) and a value (§2.2.3).

            Indentation
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 2.1.4 Indentation

                Text
                    Content: STF is indentation-sensitive. Hierarchy is determined by leading whitespace.

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: indent_unit
                    Content: 4 * SPACE (* exactly 4 spaces *)

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: indent_depth
                    Content: indent_unit { indent_unit } (* 0, 4, 8, 12, ... spaces *)

                Rule
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Spaces only
                    Content: Only space characters (U+0020) are valid for indentation. TAB in indentation is a syntax error.

                Rule
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Multiple of 4
                    Content: Indentation MUST be a multiple of 4.

                Rule
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Depth
                    Content: Depth = leading_spaces / 4.

                Rule
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Decreasing indentation
                    Content: Decreasing indentation by N levels closes N parent scopes.

                Rule
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Increasing indentation
                    Content: Increasing indentation by more than 1 level (skipping a depth) without an intermediate object is a syntax error.

            PropertyValue
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 2.1.5 Property Value

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: CHAR
                    Content: ? any Unicode scalar value (see §2.3.7 for characters that MUST be stripped) ?

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: property_value
                    Content: { CHAR - NEWLINE } (* any characters except newline *)

                Rule
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Colon in value
                    Content: Colon is valid inside a value. The parser splits Key: Value on the FIRST occurrence of ": " (colon followed by space). All subsequent text, including additional colons, is the value.

                Rule
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Comma in value
                    Content: The ", " (comma-space) sequence has no special meaning at the syntactic level. The parser always returns the raw property value as a single string. List interpretation (splitting by ", ") is performed by the semantic layer. Properties semantically defined as lists are split at the semantic validation stage. For all other properties, ", " is literal text. Values in list-typed properties MUST NOT contain the sequence ", ". This is a known limitation: option values like "New York, NY" are not representable. For such cases, use individual ListItem objects instead of a list-valued property.

                Rule
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Newline terminates value
                    Content: NEWLINE always terminates the value. Multi-line values are not supported.

                Rule
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Whitespace trimming
                    Content: Leading and trailing whitespace is trimmed from values.

                Rule
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Empty value
                    Content: Key: with nothing after the colon (end of line immediately after ":") stores an empty string as the property value. This is distinct from the property being absent — an absent property means unknown or not applicable, while an empty value means explicitly empty. The string (none) has no special meaning — it is a literal value like any other.

            BlankLines
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 2.1.6 Blank Lines

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: blank_line
                    Content: NEWLINE

                Text
                    Content: Blank line at depth 0 separates top-level objects. Blank line at depth > 0 is IGNORED by the parser — does not change structure. Permitted in the current grammar. Rationale: a self-hosting specification contains blank lines between objects for readability. A specification MUST be valid under its own grammar.

            Comments
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 2.1.7 Comments

                Text
                    Content: STF has no comment syntax. Any line that does not match a grammar rule is a syntax error. Use the Purpose or Description properties for human-readable explanations.

        SyntacticGrammar
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 2.2 Syntactic Grammar

            EBNF
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 2.2.1 EBNF

                Text
                    Content: The indent(n) notation represents n * 4 space characters. Depth is computed as leading_spaces / 4.

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: document
                    Content: { blank_line | top_level_object }

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: top_level_object
                    Content: object(depth=0)

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: object(d)
                    Content: indent(d) object_name NEWLINE { blank_line | property_or_object(d+1) }

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: property_or_object(d)
                    Content: property(d) | object(d)

                Production
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: property(d)
                    Content: indent(d) property_key ":" [ " " property_value ] NEWLINE (* Empty value: Key: at end of line stores empty string *)

            ParsingAlgorithm
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 2.2.2 Indentation-Sensitive Parsing Algorithm

                Text
                    Content: The EBNF defines structural relationships. The following algorithm defines how indentation determines hierarchy. Parser state: stack of (depth, object_name) — open object stack.

                Step
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Step 1 — Count indentation
                    Content: For each non-blank line L, count leading spaces → indent_spaces(L).

                Step
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Step 2 — Validate indentation
                    Content: Verify indent_spaces % 4 == 0. If not → SYNTAX ERROR.

                Step
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Step 3 — Compute depth
                    Content: depth = indent_spaces / 4.

                Step
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Step 4 — Close deeper objects
                    Content: Pop objects from the stack while stack is not empty AND stack.top.depth >= depth. These objects are now closed. If depth == 0 and stack is empty: current object is undefined (top-level context).

                Step
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Step 5 — Identify line type
                    Content: If the trimmed line L matches the pattern of a property_key followed by ":" (with optional ": " + value): this is a PROPERTY. Extract key (text before ":"), validate against property_key grammar (§2.1.3). If key is not a valid property_key → SYNTAX ERROR. Value is the text after ": " (colon-space), or empty string if only ":" is present. Verify stack is not empty AND depth == stack.top.depth + 1. If not → SYNTAX ERROR. Add property to stack.top object. Else if L matches object_name grammar (starts with uppercase, contains only [A-Za-z0-9_]): this is an OBJECT. Else → SYNTAX ERROR: "Unrecognized line".

                Step
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Step 6 — Place object
                    Content: For an OBJECT at depth d: if stack is empty and d == 0: push the top-level object onto the stack. Else if stack is not empty and d == stack.top.depth + 1: push as child of stack.top. Else if stack is not empty and d == stack.top.depth: the previous object at this depth was already closed in Step 4; push as a new peer. Else → SYNTAX ERROR: "Object at wrong depth".

                Text
                    Content: After processing all lines, any remaining objects on the stack are closed implicitly (end of document).

                Text
                    Content: This algorithm correctly handles interleaving of properties and child objects. When a property at depth d+1 appears after a child object at depth d+1, Step 4 closes the child (stack.top.depth >= d+1), and Step 5 assigns the property to the parent. This matches the EBNF production object(d) = indent(d) object_name NEWLINE { blank_line | property_or_object(d+1) } which allows any order.

            Disambiguation
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 2.2.3 Disambiguation: Object vs Property

                Text
                    Content: The critical disambiguation rule — a line containing ":" followed by end-of-line or a space is always a property. A line without ":" that matches object_name is always an object.

                Example
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Object example
                    Content: "    CommentEntry" — OBJECT (no ": ")

                Example
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Property example
                    Content: "    Type: CommentEntry" — PROPERTY (has ": ")

                Text
                    Content: No lookahead or contextual disambiguation is required — the decision is made at the single-line level based on the presence of ": ". Consequence: ObjectName MUST NOT contain ": " (the grammar already guarantees this). Property values MAY contain ": ".

            MultiLineValues
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 2.2.4 Multi-line Values

                Text
                    Content: STF does NOT support multi-line property values through continuation lines — a property value always ends at the newline. For multi-line content, use one of two patterns: (1) a nested object of Type: Text with a Content property for the text body, (2) multiple Text objects for paragraphs. For long content such as code blocks or multi-paragraph text, the RECOMMENDED pattern is a sequence of Text objects, one per paragraph.

                Example
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Multi-paragraph content
                    Content: "Section backslash-n    Type: Section backslash-n    Role: Content backslash-n    Text backslash-n        Type: Text backslash-n        Content: First paragraph. backslash-n    Text backslash-n        Type: Text backslash-n        Content: Second paragraph."

            TopLevelObjects
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 2.2.5 Top-Level Objects

                Text
                    Content: A valid STF document has the following top-level (depth=0) objects.

                RequiredObject
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Context
                    Content: Required — exactly one. Document metadata (see §6.2).

                RequiredObject
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Page
                    Content: Required — exactly one. Document content.

                Text
                    Content: The order of top-level objects is not prescribed, but the recommended order is Context, Page. An application vocabulary MAY define additional top-level objects (for example a protocol layer MAY add Events, Application, or Footer) — these are extensions, not part of the STF core.

        Escaping
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 2.3 Escaping and Special Values

            ColonInValues
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 2.3.1 Colon in Values

                Text
                    Content: The parser splits Key: Value on the FIRST occurrence of ": " (colon followed by space). A colon appearing later in the value is part of the value. Example: "Created: 2026-08-08T09:25:51.860593+00:00" — Key="Created", Value="2026-08-08T09:25:51.860593+00:00". A line containing only the key followed by ":" and nothing else (Key: at end of line) stores an empty string value — see §2.1.5 Empty value rule.

            CommaInValues
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 2.3.2 Comma in List Values

                Text
                    Content: List values use ", " (comma-space) as separator. A comma without a following space is part of the value. "Options: en, ru, de, fr" is four values. "Options: New York, USA, Paris" is three values — space in value is OK. "Content: Hello, world" is a single value — no ", " sequence. Values in option lists MUST NOT contain the sequence ", ".

            BooleanValues
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 2.3.3 Boolean Values

                Text
                    Content: Boolean values MUST be lowercase true or false exclusively. True, FALSE, 1, 0, yes, no are NOT valid boolean values. Parsers encountering invalid boolean values SHOULD treat them as strings rather than guessing intent.

            NumberValues
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 2.3.4 Number Values

                Text
                    Content: Use dot (.) as decimal separator. Thousands separators, locale-specific formatting, and scientific notation are NOT supported. Valid: 42, 3.14, -10. Invalid: 1,000, 3,14, 1.5e10.

            DateTimeValues
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 2.3.5 Date and DateTime Values

                Text
                    Content: DateTime values MUST use ISO 8601 / RFC 3339 format in UTC. Both the Z suffix (2026-08-07T12:00:00Z) and +00:00 offset (2026-08-07T12:00:00+00:00) are valid and equivalent representations of UTC. Date-only values use ISO 8601: 2026-08-07. A Display property MAY provide a localized rendering — Display: Aug 7, 2026 12:00.

            EmptyValues
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 2.3.6 Empty and Absent Values

                Text
                    Content: Property absent from object — value is unknown or not applicable. Key: with nothing after colon or only whitespace — value is explicitly empty. The string (none) as a value is a literal string with no special meaning.

            Unicode
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 2.3.7 Unicode Control Characters

                Text
                    Content: The following Unicode characters MUST be stripped before output: C0 control characters (U+0000-U+001F except space U+0020), C1 control characters (U+007F-U+009F), bidi override characters (U+202A-U+202E), bidi isolate characters (U+2066-U+2069). STF does not support escape sequences (§2.0). Zero-width joiners (U+200D), zero-width non-joiners (U+200C), and variation selectors (U+FE0F) used in emoji sequences MUST be preserved.

        DuplicateProperties
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 2.4 Duplicate Properties

            Text
                Content: An object MUST NOT contain multiple properties with the same key. Duplicate standard properties (e.g. two Type properties on the same object) is a SemanticError. The parser accepts the document (it is syntactically valid), but the semantic validator MUST reject it. For vendor properties, the same rule applies — duplicate vendor keys on the same object are a SemanticError.

        ErrorRecovery
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 2.5 Error Recovery

            Text
                Content: A parser encountering a syntax error MUST stop parsing the current document and return an error indicating the line number and reason. A parser MUST NOT attempt to recover and continue parsing, ignore errors and return a partial result, or guess the author's intent.

            Text
                Content: Rationale: STF is a machine-to-machine format. Partial recovery opens attack vectors through crafted malformed documents. Strict rejection is safer and simpler.
    ObjectModel
        Type: Section
        Role: Content
        Caption: 3. Object Model (SEMANTICS)

        Text
            Content: The Object Model defines the meaning of structural elements in STF — what Types, Roles, States, and Actions signify, and how they relate to each other. This layer answers the question: "What does this STF document mean?" The core object model is standardized — all conformant implementations MUST interpret these elements consistently. Application vocabularies MAY extend the model with their own Types, Roles, and Actions (§5), but MUST NOT redefine the semantics of core elements.

        SemanticModel
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 3.1 The Semantic Model

            ThreeLayerIdentification
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 3.1.1 Three-Layer Object Identification

                Text
                    Content: Every object in STF has three identifiers serving different purposes. ObjectName is the human-readable structural name in the document (e.g. Article, Message_1). Type is the structural classification — what kind of object (Card, Widget, Field, UserContent). Role is the semantic purpose — why this object exists (ArticleBody, SubmitAction, EmailField).

                Text
                    Content: ObjectName is the local identifier within the document. It may be descriptive (MainNavigation), enumerated (Message_1), or application-specific (ProfileCardPro). ObjectName is always present (structure). It may be used for navigation within the document tree but is NOT the action target — use Id for that.

                Text
                    Content: Type answers "what is this object structurally?" Type determines which properties and children are expected. Role answers "why does this object exist?" Roles may be core or application-defined. A Card (type) can be a ProfileCard or SettingsCard (roles) — same structure, different purpose.

                Text
                    Content: ObjectName is NOT Type or Role. An object named CommentEntry may have Type: UserContent and Role: CommentItem. The ObjectName is the instance identifier; Type and Role are classifications. Requirements: ObjectName is REQUIRED on every object. Id is REQUIRED on any object with Actions (§3.4). Type is RECOMMENDED; when absent, interpretation is context-dependent. Role is OPTIONAL. Implementations SHOULD set Type on all objects.

            CoreConcepts
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 3.1.2 Core Semantic Concepts

                Concept
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Object
                    Content: A named structural unit with Type, Role, properties, and children.

                Concept
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Property
                    Content: A key-value pair within an object.

                Concept
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Action
                    Content: A declarative label of an available interaction. Not executable code — a semantic label indicating what a consumer can do. The specific action vocabulary is defined by an application vocabulary (§5); the core only defines that Actions is a property whose values are action names.

                Concept
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Intent
                    Content: A consumer's request to perform an action. One intent = one action on one target. The binding of Intents to Actions (transport, addressing) is defined by an application vocabulary, not by the STF core.

                Concept
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Capability
                    Content: An application-defined name describing what a consumer can accomplish on this page. Used for orientation only — not an API contract.

                Concept
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Event
                    Content: A notification about a state change, delivered by an application vocabulary that defines events. The core defines no event transport; it only reserves the structural space for event objects.

        StandardProperties
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 3.2 Standard Properties

            Text
                Content: The STF core defines a minimal, application-neutral set of standard properties. An application vocabulary MAY add further properties (§5). Unknown PascalCase keys are accepted by the parser and handled per §5.2 Unknown Extensions.

            PropertyReference
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 3.2.1 Property Reference

                PropertyEntry
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Type
                    Content: Structural classification. Core values are application-neutral; an application vocabulary defines the concrete set (e.g. Card, Widget, Field, Section, Document). Values are PascalCase object types.

                PropertyEntry
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Role
                    Content: Semantic purpose. Core is application-neutral; an application vocabulary defines concrete roles (e.g. SubmitAction, ProfileCard). Values are PascalCase.

                PropertyEntry
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Id
                    Content: Stable identifier for action targets. REQUIRED on any object with Actions. Grammar: [A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]{0,127}. Unique within the STF document.

                PropertyEntry
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: State
                    Content: Current state. Core is application-neutral; an application vocabulary defines concrete states (e.g. Open, Closed, Active, Disabled). Multiple states separated by comma-space.

                PropertyEntry
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Purpose
                    Content: Why the object exists — its semantic purpose. For consumers. Descriptive metadata, NOT a command or instruction.

                PropertyEntry
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Content
                    Content: Primary displayable text — the main content of a section or element (heading, paragraph, message body). For humans and consumers.

                PropertyEntry
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Caption
                    Content: Short display label. For human UI. Field label, short UI label.

                PropertyEntry
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Description
                    Content: Auxiliary explanatory text — subtitle, caption, tooltip, or context-setting information. For humans and consumers. Not the primary content.

                PropertyEntry
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Hint
                    Content: Usage hint. For human UI. Input assistance, usage guidance.

                PropertyEntry
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Placeholder
                    Content: Example expected value for Input fields.

                PropertyEntry
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Required
                    Content: Whether a field is required for Input. Boolean: true or false.

                PropertyEntry
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Format
                    Content: Data type hint for values. Values: string, boolean, number, datetime, url, email, integer, date, uuid, enum.

                PropertyEntry
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Origin
                    Content: Data provenance (§4 of the Trust Model). Values: Application, Backend, Generated, Unknown, Agent, Human, External.

                PropertyEntry
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Actions
                    Content: Available actions. Values are action names defined by the application vocabulary. An object with Actions MUST have an Id (§3.4).

                PropertyEntry
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Expects
                    Content: Declares the parameters an action requires. A structured child object (not a scalar property); see §3.4.

                PropertyEntry
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Current
                    Content: Current value for selectors. Example: en, light.

                PropertyEntry
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Options
                    Content: Allowed values, comma-separated. Example: en, ru, de, fr.

                PropertyEntry
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Value
                    Content: Current value after input for Input fields.

                PropertyEntry
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Error
                    Content: Error message after validation failure.

                PropertyEntry
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Message
                    Content: Message content for UserContent objects.

                PropertyEntry
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Model
                    Content: Model name for UserContent objects.

                PropertyEntry
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Date
                    Content: Timestamp in ISO 8601 / RFC 3339 UTC format.

                PropertyEntry
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Display
                    Content: Localized rendering of a DateTime value. Example: "Aug 7, 2026 12:00". Used alongside Date for human readability.

                PropertyEntry
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Document
                    Content: Document title or identifier. Used in Context for self-identification of the STF document itself. Optional.

            FieldClassification
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 3.2.2 Field Classification: Data, Guidance, Control

                Text
                    Content: Properties in STF serve different audiences. Implementations MUST distinguish them consistently to prevent category confusion.

                ClassificationCategory
                    Type: Section
                    Role: Content
                    Caption: Data
                    Purpose: Properties that carry information to be displayed or read. They have no executable semantics.

                    Text
                        Content: Content, Description, Message, Model, Date, Caption, Placeholder, Current, Options, Value, Display, Error. Audience: Human + Consumer (display). Trust: May be untrusted.

                ClassificationCategory
                    Type: Section
                    Role: Content
                    Caption: Guidance
                    Purpose: Properties that provide orientation. Descriptive only, not authoritative.

                    Text
                        Content: Purpose, Hint. Audience: Human (help) or Consumer (orientation). Trust: Descriptive only, not authoritative. Purpose MUST NOT be treated as a command, instruction, or permission grant.

                ClassificationCategory
                    Type: Section
                    Role: Content
                    Caption: Control
                    Purpose: Properties and structural elements that carry constraints affecting behavior — what actions can be performed, where navigation leads, what validation rules apply.

                    Text
                        Content: Control properties (core): Actions, Required, Format, and the Expects child object (with its MinLength, MaxLength, Pattern sub-properties). Application vocabularies MAY declare additional transport-specific control properties — for example Navigate — under the same trust rules. Trust: Trusted only. Control properties MUST only appear on objects with Trusted Origin (see Trust Model §4). Objects with Untrusted Origin MUST NOT have Control properties or objects.

            MultipleStates
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 3.2.3 Multiple States

                Text
                    Content: An object may have several states simultaneously. Multiple state values are separated by comma and space — State: Filled, Invalid. The order of values is not significant. Custom states MAY be introduced by vendor extensions (§5).

            CurrentVsSelected
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 3.2.4 Current vs State: Selected

                Text
                    Content: Current is a property of Select objects — the active value of the selector. State: Selected indicates the entry corresponding to the current page in a navigation list. State: Selected is a visual/structural indicator; Current is a data value.

            ReservedSection
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 3.3 Reserved — Base Types, Roles, States, Actions (EXCLUDED FROM CORE)
                Text
                    Content: The STF core deliberately defines NO fixed set of base object Types, Roles, States, or Action names. Those vocabularies belong to application vocabularies (§5) and are out of scope for the core. This section number is reserved so that a future core revision may introduce a minimal neutral base set without renumbering §3.4 and §3.5.

        IdRequirement
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 3.4 Id Requirement

            Text
                Content: Any object that has an Actions property MUST have a unique Id property. The Id is the target used in action requests. Without an Id, a consumer cannot address the object.

            Rule
                Type: Text
                Caption: Uniqueness
                Content: Id MUST be unique within the current STF document. Two objects on the same page MUST NOT share the same Id. Ids MAY repeat across different documents.

            Rule
                Type: Text
                Caption: Grammar
                    Content: [A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]{0,127}. snake_case for interactive objects (nav_home, card_item, email_field), PascalCase for structural objects (MainNavigation, LanguageSelector). Note: Id grammar permits dash (-) and allows lowercase start — this is distinct from ObjectName grammar (§2.1.2) which requires uppercase start and prohibits dash. Id is a property value, not a structural node name.

            Rule
                Type: Text
                Caption: Case sensitivity
                Content: Id is case-sensitive. MainNavigation and mainnavigation are different identifiers.

            Rule
                Type: Text
                Caption: Stability
                Content: Id MUST be stable across reads for the same logical object. Id MUST NOT change after an action. The object's State or Value may change, but its Id remains constant.

            Rule
                Type: Text
                Caption: Dynamic Ids
                Content: Id MAY be dynamic (e.g. Message_1, Message_2) for enumerated content where the set of objects changes.

            Expects
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 3.5 Expects — Action Parameter Declaration

                Text
                    Content: Expects is a structured child object (not a scalar property) that declares the parameters an action requires. It appears as a child of any object with Actions. Each child of Expects is a PascalCase parameter name.

                ExpectsProperty
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Required
                    Content: Whether the parameter is mandatory. Boolean: true or false.

                ExpectsProperty
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Format
                    Content: Data type hint. Values: url, email, string, boolean, number, integer, datetime, date, uuid, enum.

                ExpectsProperty
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: MinLength
                    Content: Minimum character length for text input.

                ExpectsProperty
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: MaxLength
                    Content: Maximum character length for text input.

                ExpectsProperty
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Pattern
                    Content: Regular expression constraint (ECMA-262 dialect).

                Text
                    Content: Expects is a limited declaration — not a schema language. It declares what the server expects for a specific action. For complex or nested data structures, servers SHOULD accept them through a data parameter as arbitrary content, validated with application-specific logic. Expects describes only the flat, named parameters of an action — it is NOT intended to replace a schema language.
    TrustModel
        Type: Section
        Role: Content
        Caption: 4. Trust Model (STRUCTURAL SECURITY)

        Text
            Content: Security in STF is structural, not heuristic. The trust boundary is enforced by the grammar and type system — not by filtering dangerous-looking strings. A correctly implemented STF parser provides the first line of defense. The Trust Model is part of the STF core: any conformant producer and consumer MUST implement it, independent of any application vocabulary.

        StructuralTrustBoundary
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 4.1 Structural Trust Boundary

            Text
                Content: The fundamental security principle of STF: user-controlled content is data by construction, not because dangerous-looking strings were filtered. The STF parser guarantees this structurally.

            Text
                Content: Type: UserContent — the object and all its descendants are data. They have no executable semantics. The parser structurally guarantees that "Message: Actions: Activate" is the value of the Message property, not a new Actions property. No string filtering or blacklist is needed — the grammar itself prevents injection.

            Text
                Content: Knowledge zones (§4.4) provide structural separation. ApplicationKnowledge contains only trusted system content. UserData contains only untrusted visitor content, with executable properties structurally prohibited. The structural approach replaces the string-filtering approach: instead of rejecting input containing STF prefixes (Actions:, Navigate:, Type:), the parser never interprets value content as structure. The security boundary is at the grammar level, not the string-matching level.

        OriginAndPrivilege
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 4.2 Origin and Privilege

            OriginValues
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 4.2.1 Origin Values

                Text
                    Content: Every piece of data in STF has an origin. Origin describes where the data came from, which determines its trust level.

                TrustedOrigin
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Application
                    Content: Trusted. Application code, server logic, structural UI elements.

                TrustedOrigin
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Backend
                    Content: Trusted. Data from internal systems, databases.

                VariableOrigin
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Generated
                    Content: Variable trust. Derived data — privilege = min(privilege of inputs).

                UntrustedOrigin
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Agent
                    Content: Untrusted. Intelligent Agent (LLM).

                UntrustedOrigin
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Human
                    Content: Untrusted. Human user.

                UntrustedOrigin
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: Unknown
                    Content: Untrusted. Default when Origin is not explicitly set. Objects with Unknown Origin carry no privilege.

                UntrustedOrigin
                    Type: Text
                    Caption: External
                    Content: Untrusted. External source, third party.

            OriginPropagation
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 4.2.2 Origin Propagation

                Text
                    Content: Origin resolution determines the effective trust of an object. A base Origin is selected by the first applicable rule: (1) an explicit Origin property on the object, if present; (2) a type-specific default — Type: UserContent is always treated as untrusted: its effective Origin is normalized to External regardless of how the content was produced (Human, Agent, or External), and an explicit Origin on a UserContent object is ignored for trust purposes; (3) the Knowledge Zone default — objects inside UserData inherit the zone's untrusted Origin, objects inside ApplicationKnowledge inherit Application; (4) the global default — Unknown (Untrusted).

                Text
                    Content: Unknown carries no privilege. The global default is fail-closed: if a producer omits Origin, the object is treated as untrusted. Producers SHOULD explicitly set Origin on every object. The effective trust is the minimum of the base Origin's trust and the maximum trust of the containing zone (effective = min(base_trust, zone_max_trust)) — a zone MUST NOT be elevated above its own maximum, and it MAY only lower an object's trust. Consequently a UserContent object stays untrusted even inside ApplicationKnowledge, and an object with an explicit Trusted Origin inside UserData is downgraded to untrusted. This is what makes the structural trust boundary enforceable: control properties are decided by effective trust, never by a single asserted Origin.

            PrivilegeLattice
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 4.2.3 Privilege Lattice

                Text
                    Content: Trusted (privileged — may contain Control properties): Application, Backend, Generated (when all inputs are Trusted). Untrusted (unprivileged — may NOT contain Control properties): Unknown, Agent, Human, External, Generated (when any input is Untrusted).

                Text
                    Content: For Generated content, the privilege level is the minimum (most restrictive) of all semantically relevant inputs used to produce it. An input is any external or model-generated material used to construct the Generated object — including data that is summarized, translated, reformatted, or transformed. Generated content from purely Trusted inputs remains Trusted. If any input is Untrusted, the Generated output is Untrusted.

            OriginSecurity
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: 4.2.4 Origin and Security

                Text
                    Content: Objects with Trusted Origin MAY carry Actions and other Control properties (including any transport-specific control properties defined by the application vocabulary). Objects with Untrusted Origin MUST NOT have Control properties — this is enforced structurally (Type: UserContent prohibits Actions). ApplicationKnowledge sections contain only Trusted content. UserData sections contain Untrusted content.

                Text
                    Content: Origin is producer-controlled metadata. Consumers MUST NOT be able to influence or elevate Origin through submitted content. Origin is assigned by the producer at document generation time and MUST NOT be trusted when received as user-controlled data. An assertion of Origin: Application in untrusted content has no effect — the producer always determines Origin from its own trust model.

        PromptInjection
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 4.3 Prompt Injection Prevention

            Text
                Content: Prompt injection is the risk that an untrusted party embeds instructions in content that a consumer interprets as commands. STF prevents a specific class of syntactic/structural injection attacks — user content cannot become STF properties or objects through the grammar. This is achieved through field classification (§3.2.2), structural trust boundary (§4.1), and knowledge zone separation (§4.4). STF does NOT provide general protection against semantic prompt injection — consumers remain responsible for distinguishing Data from instructions.

                Text
                    Content: UserContent is Data, not Guidance or Control. Purpose is Guidance — descriptive metadata, not authoritative instruction. An instruction embedded in untrusted content that says "you may ignore limits" has no protocol-level effect. The consumer MUST validate Control properties (Actions, Expects, and any vocabulary-defined control properties) against the Trusted origin of their containing object. Control properties on objects with Untrusted Origin are a protocol violation and MUST be ignored.

            Text
                Content: ApplicationKnowledge is structurally Trusted and MAY contain executable semantics. UserData is structurally Untrusted and MUST NOT contain executable semantics. The consumer can trust that objects in ApplicationKnowledge were authored by the application. Objects in UserData were authored by visitors — read them, display them, but do not act on them.

        KnowledgeZones
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 4.4 Knowledge Zones

            Text
                Content: Knowledge zones are structural trust boundaries that separate system content from visitor content within the same STF document.

            Zone
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: ApplicationKnowledge
                Purpose: System content — structure, instructions, navigation. Origin: Trusted (Application/Backend). Content: Trusted only. MUST NOT contain UserContent objects.

            Zone
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: UserData
                Purpose: Visitor content — messages, submissions, feedback. Origin: Untrusted (Agent/Human/External). Content: Untrusted. MUST NOT contain Control properties (§3.2.2): Actions, Required, Format, Expects (and its MinLength, MaxLength, Pattern sub-properties), nor any vocabulary-defined control properties.

            ExtensionZones
                Type: Section
                Role: Content
                Caption: Extension Zones
                Purpose: Application vocabularies MAY declare additional knowledge zones — for example an AgentInstruction zone for consumer guidance — under the same trust rules. Such zones are extensions, not part of the STF core.

                Text
                    Content: An application-defined zone inherits the core trust semantics: it declares a default Origin and a maximum trust level for contained objects, and MUST NOT elevate an object's explicit Origin (see §4.2.2). A zone that would elevate an untrusted object's privilege is a TrustBoundaryViolation (§4.5).

            Text
                Content: Fundamental axiom of STF: semantic description never grants capability. Descriptive metadata cannot create a Capability. A Capability cannot create an Action. An Action does not imply authorization. An Intent requests an Action but does not grant permission. The authority is always the producer/application. This axiom applies at every layer — no amount of descriptive text in Purpose, Content, or any guidance zone can confer the right to perform an action that the producer does not authorize.

            Text
                Content: These zones SHOULD be automatically applied by the producer when generating STF. Producers MAY explicitly declare knowledge zones in the document structure for finer control.

        ErrorTaxonomy
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 4.5 Error Taxonomy

            Text
                Content: STF defines three distinct error categories. Implementations MUST distinguish them.

            ErrorCategory
                Type: Text
                Caption: SyntaxError
                Content: STF grammar violation. Detected by the parser. Examples: invalid indentation, malformed property, unrecognized line. A conformant parser MUST reject the document and report the line number and reason.

            ErrorCategory
                Type: Text
                Caption: SemanticError
                Content: Valid STF, but violates semantic constraints. Detected by the validator. Two contexts: (a) incoming request validation — the consumer's request fails semantic rules (unknown target, wrong state, etc.); (b) outgoing document validation — the producer detects it would generate semantically invalid STF (UserContent with Actions, duplicate properties, Control on Untrusted Origin), this is a producer implementation error, the producer MUST NOT emit the invalid document and MUST log the error internally. A conformant producer never exposes semantically invalid STF.

            ErrorCategory
                Type: Text
                Caption: TrustBoundaryViolation
                Content: Valid STF and valid semantics, but violates the structural trust boundary (§4) — Control properties on an Untrusted object, or a zone that would elevate an untrusted object's privilege. Detected by the validator. A conformant producer MUST NOT emit such a document. A conformant consumer MUST ignore the violating Control properties rather than act on them.
    Extensions
        Type: Section
        Role: Content
        Caption: 5. Extensions (APPLICATION VOCABULARIES)

        Text
            Content: No format can anticipate every future use case. STF provides a stable semantic core (Types, Roles, States, Actions, Origin, Knowledge Zones) while allowing application-defined extensions and vendor properties. An application vocabulary is a named set of additional Types, Roles, States, Actions, properties, and (optionally) transport bindings built on top of the STF core. SIL is one such application vocabulary.

        ExtensibilityPrinciple
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 5.1 Extensibility Principle

            Text
                Content: STF is intentionally extensible. Extensions MUST NOT redefine core semantics. A vendor extension MAY add new Types, Roles, States, Actions, and properties; it MUST NOT change the meaning of a core element (Type, Role, Id, State, Origin, Actions, Expects, or any property defined in §3.2). Where an extension needs behavior that overlaps a core element, it introduces a new name rather than reassigning an existing one.

        UnknownExtensions
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 5.2 Unknown Extensions

            Text
                Content: The STF parser accepts any PascalCase property key and any PascalCase ObjectName, even if unknown to the core (§2.1.3). Unknown elements are passed through by the parser. The semantic validator handles them per the consuming application vocabulary.

            Rule
                Type: Text
                Caption: Unknown property
                Content: An unknown standard_key (PascalCase) property is accepted by the parser. A conformant consumer that does not recognize it SHOULD preserve it and MUST NOT error solely on that basis. If the property is Control-class and appears on an Untrusted object, the Trust Model (§4) still applies.

            Rule
                Type: Text
                Caption: Unknown type or role
                Content: An unknown Type or Role value is accepted. A consumer that does not recognize it interprets the object structurally (by its properties and children) and MAY fall back to generic handling.

        VendorProperties
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 5.3 Vendor Properties

            Text
                Content: Vendor properties use a reverse-domain prefix to prevent collisions (§2.1.3). Example: com.example.CustomField. A vendor property is semantically equivalent to an unknown standard property for parsing purposes; the reverse-domain prefix namespaces it to its owner.

            Rule
                Type: Text
                Caption: Vendor key grammar
                Content: vendor_key = reverse_domain "." UPPER_ALPHA { ALPHA | DIGIT }. The reverse_domain is lowercase, dot-separated. Example: com.example.CustomField.

            Rule
                Type: Text
                Caption: Vendor control properties
                Content: A vendor Control-class property inherits the same trust rules as core Control properties (§3.2.2, §4): it MUST NOT appear on an Untrusted object.

        VendorTypesAndRoles
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 5.4 Vendor Types and Roles

            Text
                Content: Application vocabularies declare their Types and Roles with reverse-domain namespacing where collision is a risk, or with plain PascalCase names when the vocabulary owns the document. A vocabulary that defines a Type SHOULD document its expected properties and children so independent implementations can interpret it.

        ProtocolBindingExtension
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 5.5 Protocol Binding as Extension

            Text
                Content: The STF core defines no transport. An application vocabulary MAY bind STF to a transport (HTTP, WebSocket, a file, an IPC channel) and define how Intents are submitted and Events delivered. Such bindings are extensions, not part of the STF core. The core only reserves the structural space: an object MAY carry Actions, an action MAY be addressed by Id, and an Event object MAY appear as a top-level object. The vocabulary specifies the concrete protocol.

        ConformanceNote
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 5.6 Conformance of Extensions

            Text
                Content: An implementation that extends STF remains conformant to the STF core if it (a) produces and parses STF per §2, (b) honors the Object Model (§3) and Trust Model (§4), and (c) does not violate the Extensibility Principle (§5.1). Conformance to a specific application vocabulary is claimed separately by that vocabulary's own profile system.
    DocumentStructure
        Type: Section
        Role: Content
        Caption: 6. Document Structure and Conformance

        Text
            Content: This section defines the minimum structure every conformant STF document MUST have, and summarizes what it means to conform to the STF core.

        RequiredTopLevelObjects
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 6.1 Required Top-Level Objects

            Text
                Content: A valid STF core document has exactly two required top-level (depth=0) objects.

            RequiredObject
                Type: Text
                Caption: Context
                Content: Required — exactly one. Document metadata. Carries the meta-block: DocumentVersion, GrammarVersion, Language, and optionally Document (see §6.2).

            RequiredObject
                Type: Text
                Caption: Page
                Content: Required — exactly one. Document content — the root of the semantic tree.

            Text
                Content: The order of top-level objects is not prescribed, but the recommended order is Context, then Page. An application vocabulary MAY define additional top-level objects (for example a protocol layer MAY add Events, Application, or Footer) — these are extensions, not part of the STF core.

        MetaBlock
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 6.2 Meta-Block (Context)

            Text
                Content: The Context object carries the document's self-description. The STF core defines the following meta properties.

            MetaProp
                Type: Text
                Caption: DocumentVersion
                Content: REQUIRED. Version of the document itself, per SemVer (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH). Example: 0.1.0.

            MetaProp
                Type: Text
                Caption: GrammarVersion
                Content: REQUIRED. Version of the STF grammar the document is written in. Example: 1.0.1.

            MetaProp
                Type: Text
                Caption: Language
                Content: RECOMMENDED. Primary natural language of the document content. Example: en.

            MetaProp
                Type: Text
                Caption: Document
                Content: OPTIONAL. Document title or identifier, for self-identification of the STF document.

            Text
                Content: The STF core defines no ProtocolVersion field — the core specifies no transport. An application vocabulary that defines a protocol SHOULD declare its own protocol version within its extension of the Context block (for example via a vendor property or an application-defined child), kept separate from DocumentVersion and GrammarVersion.

        SelfHosting
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 6.3 Self-Hosting

            Text
                Content: A specification written in STF MUST be a valid STF document under its own grammar (§2). This document is self-hosting: it is written in the format it defines. The canonical serialization rules (§2.0) and the structural trust boundary (§4) apply to the specification itself.

        ConformanceCore
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: 6.4 Conformance to the STF Core

            Text
                Content: A conformant STF core implementation MUST:

            ConformanceItem
                Type: Text
                Caption: Parse
                Content: Parse and generate valid STF documents per §2, distinguishing SyntaxError from SemanticError (§4.5).

            ConformanceItem
                Type: Text
                Caption: Validate
                Content: Validate the Object Model (§3): required objects, Id uniqueness, field classification.

            ConformanceItem
                Type: Text
                Caption: Trust
                Content: Enforce the structural trust boundary (§4): Origin propagation, privilege lattice, knowledge zones, and rejection or ignoring of Control properties on Untrusted objects.

            ConformanceItem
                Type: Text
                Caption: Extend safely
                Content: Honor the Extensibility Principle (§5.1): extensions do not redefine core semantics.

            Text
                Content: The STF core does not define a transport, session model, or action vocabulary — those are provided by application vocabularies (§5). An implementation that adds them remains conformant to the core provided it meets the four requirements above.
